<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jean Decoux</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1884">1884</a>–<a href="/years/1963">1963</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Admiral Jean Decoux</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jean Decoux was a French admiral who governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">French Indochina</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> regime from 1940 to 1945. He accepted the stationing of Japanese troops in the colony while keeping French administration in place, and promoted youth, sports and local patriotic movements loyal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippe-petain">Marshal Pétain</a>. He was removed when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> seized power in March 1945 and was later tried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jean-decoux.jpg" alt="Jean Decoux" width="262" height="285" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Remerciements Jacques Decoux · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jean_Decoux_1919.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
